# Error handling

## Never swallow errors silently

An empty or log-only `catch` hides real failures from callers and turns bugs
into silent data corruption.

```typescript
// BAD
try {
  await saveTicket(ticket);
} catch (e) {}

// GOOD
try {
  await saveTicket(ticket);
} catch (e) {
  throw new Error(`Failed to save ticket ${ticket.id}: ${e instanceof Error ? e.message : e}`);
}
```

## Preserve context when you rethrow

A caught error should gain information as it propagates, not lose it. Include
the operation, the relevant id/input, and the original error — do not replace
it with a generic message.

```php
// BAD
catch (Exception $e) {
    throw new Exception('Something went wrong');
}

// GOOD
catch (Exception $e) {
    throw new Exception("Failed to resolve schedule for ticket #{$ticket->getId()}: " . $e->getMessage(), 0, $e);
}
```

## Distinguish expected failures from bugs

Expected failures (validation errors, not-found, permission denied) should
return/throw typed, specific results the caller can branch on. Unexpected
failures (nulls where a contract guarantees a value, assertion violations)
should fail fast and loud — do not paper over them with a fallback default.
